Transition pitch accent models are commonly used to analyze the phonology of languages. However, their application in natural language processing for code-mixed languages has been overlooked. Recent advances in deep learning have enabled the successful training of transition pitch accent models for these languages. Nonetheless, the issue persists of transition boundary detection, which is crucial for determining the accent timing. One method to address this is to use a pre-trained multi-task classifier that predicts the transition point (PESTO) along with the accent pattern, language identification, and script type as separate outputs. The proposed approach leverages the relative position of phonological segments through dynamic positional encoding. The effectiveness of PESTO is demonstrated in various evaluations, including binary classification, multi-class classification, and metric classification tasks. Moreover, it is shown that the proposed model can be efficiently trained on relatively small datasets. Our analysis includes a preliminary evaluation of PESTO on isiNdebele-Xhosa, a language with intricate phonology characteristics. The potential benefits of the model are also contemplated in terms of its ability to enhance existing language models with phonological awareness. The transition point data generated by PESTO has been made publicly accessible. Furthermore, the code used for data processing and model training is available under the MIT license at the specified repository. 

