This research centers around developing innovative strategies to guide a swarm of autonomous drones, modeled as mobile agents, around dynamic crowds of tiny objects, simulated as a shifting mass within a two-dimensional space. Our primary goal is to design algorithms that maintain sparse representation of the crowd within a constant distance from the drones, thereby ensuring the crowd's continued formation and preventing mid-flight collisions. To achieve this, we introduce a registration algorithm that efficiently matches the shape of the crowd with a simplified geometric structure, allowing us to maintain a basic spacial pattern with all entities at a set distance. Leveraging this structure, we decompose the crowd into distinct regions and derive a sparse representation of their spatial relationships. Our results demonstrate that this approach allows for collision-free navigation around shifting masses, while preserving their underlying structure. Additionally, we analyzed the algorithm's computational complexity and found it to be proportional to the number of entities in the crowd. Consequently, we explored a set of approximation algorithms that offer more efficient computation but reduced accuracy. Our research combines elements of distributed registration, approximation algorithms, and the design of efficient protocols for autonomous systems. Our findings have implications for designing sparse sensors, such as micro aerial vehicles, to gather information on shifting crowds, while ensuring their continued formation. A demonstration video is available at https://youtu.be/6RQ9W9I7k3M. 

