This work focuses on utilizing three-dimensional skeletons to represent and analyze the intricate interfaces present in complex systems such as oil recovery, lung function, and fluid dynamics. Through a novel approach, we generate a skeletal model from a given density field by applying a new criterion that evaluates the interface's relevance. This is accomplished by combining the Extended Gaussian Image (EGI) technique with the skeletonization method. Our results demonstrate the effectiveness of this combination in producing high-quality skeletal representations, which can then be utilized for computational modeling and simulation purposes. We successfully apply this approach to simulate oil recovery and study the dynamics of lung diseases. Our novel skeletal models enable us to gain deeper insights into these processes. Additionally, we introduce a new metric to characterize the skeletal volume and assess its accuracy. Our work shows that skeletal models offer a valuable tool for analyzing and simulating the complex behavior of fluids and gases. 

