This research explores how human learners, when presented with brief video clips of objects moving, can accurately predict the objects' continued motion. Despite significant progress, the underlying cognitive processes involved remain poorly understood. A novel approach is taken to analyze the predictions made by a deep learning model, trained to mimic human performance on a challenging identification task. By comparing the predictions of the deep learning model with those of human subjects, the results indicate that both overestimate the likelihood of observed patterns recurring. Furthermore, when testing the deep learning model on a related classification task, the results suggest that both temporal and spatial correlations in the predictions are crucial for success. The findings imply that dynamic identification models may benefit from incorporating more nuanced theories of perception, such as those proposing the detection of causal events or meaningful patterns. By combining computational and cognitive approaches, this study makes progress towards a more comprehensive understanding of both human minimal clips and machine learning. The study also offers a new approach for evaluating the validity of trained deep learning models. Its code is publicly available on GitHub. 

